import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Install the agent workspace in DeepSeek Harness — dsh-agent-plugins-market guide"
  description="Step-by-step guide: install dsh-agent-plugins-market in a DSH profile, add a Claude Code marketplace repo as a source, and install plugin suites with skills, MCP servers, hooks and slash commands."
>
  <h1>Install the agent workspace in DeepSeek Harness</h1>
  <p>
    This guide shows how to bring Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and Kimi plugin marketplaces into DeepSeek Harness (DSH) in a few minutes.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Node.js 22+ and a DSH Web profile with the skill service; the <a href="https://github.com/Sivan757/dsh-agent-plugins-market#quick-start">README</a> lists the DSH host packages this plugin declares</li>
    <li>A terminal with <code>dsh</code>; Git for Git sources, and pnpm for GitHub or manual installation</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 1 — Install the plugin</h2>
  <p>npm registry (recommended):</p>
  <pre><code># inside a dsh profile
pnpm add dsh-agent-plugins-market

# or via the dsh CLI
dsh plugin --profile &lt;name&gt; add dsh-agent-plugins-market</code></pre>
  <p>Or install directly from GitHub:</p>
  <pre><code>dsh plugin --profile &lt;name&gt; add github:Sivan757/dsh-agent-plugins-market</code></pre>

  <h2>Step 2 — Restart dsh and open the market</h2>
  <p>
    Restart dsh, open the Web GUI, then go to <strong>Settings → Agent Plugins Market</strong>.
    npm packages contain built artifacts. GitHub installs build through <code>prepare</code> and need Node.js and pnpm. Older shells may expose a top-level page entry instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 3 — Add a marketplace source</h2>
  <p>
    In the market page, add a supported git marketplace repository URL as a source (for example a Claude Code marketplace repo with <code>.claude-plugin/marketplace.json</code>).
    No sources are preconfigured. Source ids are assigned automatically.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 4 — Install suites</h2>
  <p>
    Browse the card grid, open a suite for a preview of its skills / MCP servers / hooks / commands, then install it and ensure it is enabled.
    Installed plugins live under <code>~/.dsh/agent-plugins/.sources/&lt;sourceId&gt;/</code> (user dimension) or <code>&lt;project&gt;/.dsh/agent-plugins/.sources/&lt;sourceId&gt;/</code> (project dimension).
  </p>

  <h2>Step 5 — Verify and use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>For a suite with skills, check the Skills tab, then type <code>/</code> in chat to find its user-invocable skills.</li>
    <li>For MCP, open MCP services and resolve connection or credential notices. Credentials and overrides are edited here; suite previews are read-only.</li>
    <li>Commands become slash entries. Role delegation through <code>subagent_run</code> uses a dynamic role catalog and requires host agents, tools, LLM and subagent services.</li>
    <li>LSP requires host LSP packages and a profile exposing the LSP tool; check LSP servers for diagnostics.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>See the <a href="https://github.com/Sivan757/dsh-agent-plugins-market/blob/main/docs/guides/usage.md">usage guide</a> for host requirements and configuration.</p>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Project-dimension MCP servers are not mounted (DSH has no per-session tool scope); the project dimension covers skills and context.</li>
    <li>There is no file watcher. Local-source discovery caches results for up to 30 seconds; refresh the source to invalidate them. Project discovery has a separate five-second cache.</li>
    <li>Remote URL marketplace entries show as "remote reference" cards; add the target repo as a source to install them.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
